1	# Spec: `atlas schema inspect` (community/dev build at `/tmp/atlas_orig`)
2	
3	All observations were made by running the binary through its normal CLI only.
4	Unless stated otherwise: output was captured piped (non-TTY), exit code shown as `exit=N`.
5	Conventions below: stdout/stderr are shown separately; every fenced block is byte-verbatim.
6	
7	## Global facts
8	
9	- Version identity (`atlas version`, all on **stdout**, exit 0, stderr empty, trailing newline after last line):
10	
11	```text
12	atlas unofficial version - development
13	https://github.com/ariga/atlas/releases/latest
14	To download an official version, visit: https://atlasgo.io/getting-started#installation
15	```
16	
17	- **Community-edition / unofficial-build notice**: there is **no** separate notice printed by
18	  `schema inspect` (or by `--help` of any probed command) — not on stdout, not on stderr,
19	  not under a TTY, not on errors. The only notice-like text in the whole CLI surface observed
20	  is the `atlas version` output above ("unofficial version - development" + "To download an
21	  official version..."), which goes to **stdout** only, and only for the `version` command.
22	  `atlas license` prints a short Apache-2.0 note on stdout, exit 0, nothing on stderr.
23	- **Errors**: always printed to **stderr** as a single line `Error: <message>\n`, exit code **1**,
24	  stdout empty. (Cobra-style usage text is NOT printed on errors — only the `Error:` line.)
25	- **TTY vs piped**: no difference for `schema inspect`. Under `script -qec ... /dev/null` the
26	  bytes are identical (modulo the terminal's `\n`→`\r\n` translation). No colors, no spinner,
27	  no prompts — inspect never prompts.
28	- No trailing whitespace on any output line; HCL output contains no blank lines.
29	- `atlas help schema inspect`, `atlas schema inspect -h`, and `atlas schema inspect --help`
30	  produce byte-identical output.
31	
32	## `atlas schema inspect --help` (verbatim)
33	
34	stdout, exit 0, stderr empty:
35	
36	```text
37	'atlas schema inspect' connects to the given database and inspects its schema.
38	It then prints to the screen the schema of that database in Atlas DDL syntax. This output can be
39	saved to a file, commonly by redirecting the output to a file named with a ".hcl" suffix:
40	
41	  atlas schema inspect -u "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/dbname" > schema.hcl
42	
43	This file can then be edited and used with the `atlas schema apply` command to plan
44	and execute schema migrations against the given database. In cases where users wish to inspect
45	all multiple schemas in a given database (for instance a MySQL server may contain multiple named
46	databases), omit the relevant part from the url, e.g. "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/".
47	To select specific schemas from the databases, users may use the "--schema" (or "-s" shorthand)
48	flag.
49	
50	Usage:
51	  atlas schema inspect [flags]
52	
53	Examples:
54	  atlas schema inspect -u "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/dbname"
55	  atlas schema inspect -u "mariadb://user:pass@localhost:3306/" --schema=schemaA,schemaB -s schemaC
56	  atlas schema inspect --url "postgres://user:pass@host:port/dbname?sslmode=disable"
57	  atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://file:ex1.db?_fk=1"
58	
59	Flags:
60	  -u, --url string        [driver://username:password@address/dbname?param=value] select a resource using the URL format
61	      --dev-url string    [driver://username:password@address/dbname?param=value] select a dev database using the URL format
62	  -s, --schema strings    set schema names
63	      --exclude strings   list of glob patterns used to filter resources from applying
64	      --format string     Go template to use to format the output
65	  -h, --help              help for inspect
66	
67	Global Flags:
68	  -c, --config string        select config (project) file using URL format (default "file://atlas.hcl")
69	      --env string           set which env from the config file to use
70	      --var <name>=<value>   input variables (default [])
71	```
72	
73	Parent `atlas schema --help` (stdout, exit 0):
74	
75	```text
76	The `atlas schema` command groups subcommands working with declarative Atlas schemas.
77	
78	Usage:
79	  atlas schema [command]
80	
81	Available Commands:
82	  apply       Apply an atlas schema to a target database.
83	  clean       Removes all objects from the connected database.
84	  diff        Calculate and print the diff between two schemas.
